index b7913e8..78d2029 100644
--- a/docs/rpc.md
+++ b/docs/rpc.md
@@ -36,6 +36,40 @@ Behavior notes:
 
 ## Transport and Framing
 
+### Reconnectable local host
+
+`--rpc-socket` switches only the transport and lifetime; without it stdio RPC retains its EOF shutdown behavior.
+
+Add `--session-settings` to keep all settings edits, including native `/model` role assignments, thinking defaults, and fallback-chain edits, in a process-local native `Settings.overlay()`. The overlay is installed before auth discovery, the model registry, and session construction. Native user/project/config-overlay reads, `modelRoles.judge` and its fallback policy, credentials, and runtime storage remain shared; no auth directory or model catalog is copied. Model changes still append ordinary session `model_change` records and restore on resume. The flag does not imply `--no-session`, and omitting it retains normal native settings persistence. Runtime-only settings edits themselves do not survive process restart; resume without a fresh `--model` to restore the transcript's selected model.
+
+`get_state.settingsScope` reports the actual native write boundary (`"session"` or `"persistent"`), including worker mirrors. Clients must reject routine preset mutation unless it is `"session"`; an older host omitting the field is not isolated. Add `--model-preset <name>` with `--session-settings` to apply one native `modelPresets.<name>` snapshot after constructing the session but before listening, announcing ready, or submitting the initial prompt. A private `--config` file may supply that frozen named entry. ACP is excluded because it has no single startup session. This flag invokes the native preset application path only for that startup session; it is not a model-role alias and does not create a saved preset.
+
+`get_state.modelSettings` is `{ modelRoles, defaultThinkingLevel }` read directly from the session's effective native settings. It is independent of the active `model` and `thinkingLevel`, never a request/name echo, and contains no auth or endpoint configuration. Compare this complete readback with the frozen expected snapshot after reconnect, restart, or a lost mutation acknowledgement; preset identity alone does not establish that roles and thinking applied. Older hosts omit this field rather than fabricating a settings snapshot. An exact `--model` seed may accompany `--model-preset` so initial session construction does not depend on an unrelated saved default being available; the native full-preset application still runs last.
+
+The native SDK helper `captureModelPreset(settings)` produces the same defined-role snapshot used by saving a preset and by RPC readback. Live preset mutation uses the native session-settlement predicate, also refusing compaction, session transition, and owned running jobs.
+
+An idle isolated host accepts `{ "type": "set_model_preset", "name": "high", "preset": { "modelRoles": { "default": "provider/model", "judge": "provider/judge" }, "defaultThinkingLevel": "high" } }`. The complete snapshot is installed as a runtime-owned native preset, then the same serialized native application path used by `/modelpreset switch` applies its full roles and thinking selection. Success returns `{ name, preset, model, thinkingLevel?, settingsScope: "session" }`, where `preset` is effective native readback, not an echo. Invalid/unavailable or shadowed applications fail explicitly; persistent or busy hosts refuse. Existing native preset names and files remain unchanged.
+
+For deliberately authorized first-run creation only, call `createModelPresetIfMissing(settings, name, preset)` from `config/model-presets` on a persisted native `Settings` instance. It uses the existing native YAML write lock, rereads the raw global record under that lock, and never replaces an existing same-name key, including hidden/null/invalid entries or a concurrent external insert. Existing higher-layer entries are preserved as well. It returns `{ kind: "created" | "preserved", preset }` with authoritative native readback after flush/reload, or `{ kind: "conflict", reason }`; malformed YAML is not quarantined or repaired by this optional bootstrap path. It does not acquire the role-mutation lock, so a multi-preset initializer may hold that native lock across its own validation/batch. Routine host selection must not use a persisted instance, and ordinary unconditional `setEntry` is not a no-clobber bootstrap API.
+
+```sh
+mkdir -m 700 /private/tmp/omp-host-$UID
+omp --mode rpc-ui --rpc-socket /private/tmp/omp-host-$UID/agent.sock \
+  --session /absolute/path/agent.jsonl --cwd /absolute/project -e /absolute/extension.ts
+omp attach /private/tmp/omp-host-$UID/agent.sock
+omp attach /private/tmp/omp-host-$UID/agent.sock --read-only
+```
+
+The socket directory must already exist, be owned by the current user, have mode `0700`, and not be a symlink. The host creates a `0600` Unix socket and refuses to overwrite any existing path, including stale sockets. Remove a stale socket explicitly only after checking its owning process. Each host process owns exactly one explicit absolute session file (created if absent); existing session cwd must match `--cwd`. Session replacement/navigation commands are rejected: start another host process for another session. The absolute socket path is exported as `OMP_RPC_SOCKET` before extensions load. Extensions connecting to their own host must not await ordinary RPC commands inside `session_start`, since startup holds those commands until the hook returns.
+
+RPC clients connect directly with the existing ready/version negotiation, JSONL commands, events, responses, and v2 chunks. Request IDs, event filters (including delta projection), protocol negotiation, and subagent subscriptions are independent per connection. Use the public `RpcClient` custom `spawn` transport; its `kill()` must close only that socket and its `exited` promise must settle on socket close. `RpcClient.request(command, { id, timeoutMs })` accepts a caller-persisted request ID without replay. `onExtensionUIRequest` and `respondToUI` expose the native UI side channel.
+
+Native `attach` reuses the existing Collab guest snapshot, event, transcript, subagent, and dialog rendering over the same private endpoint; no relay or second writer of the host session is started. Its local replica has a separate identity and always uses process-local session settings, so local model menus cannot persist operator role changes. Read-only guests cannot prompt, abort, run remote agent actions, or answer dialogs. Leaving or losing the attach connection exits the attach TUI, not the host.
+
+Disconnecting any RPC/controller/TUI client does not abort the session or drop its tools or unresolved dialogs. Host-tool and URI calls go only to their registering client; a disconnected capability owner fails its active calls explicitly, and the retained definitions remain unavailable until a client explicitly re-registers them. A spectator cannot clear another owner's registration. Dialogs retain their original RPC IDs, replay on reconnect, and appear in socket-host `get_state.data.pendingUiRequests`; `hostPid` identifies the authoritative OS process. The first valid answer across RPC and writable TUI surfaces wins and cancels the other presentations. A disconnect after submission but before confirmation is uncertain: there is no replay/result journal, and callers must not resend blindly.
+
+Send `{ "type": "shutdown_host", "id": "stop-1" }` (or call `RpcClient.shutdownHost()`) for graceful persisted shutdown. It returns `session_busy` while any run, queued/admitted/background work, pending approval, host capability call, or other outstanding command remains. This includes owned persistent services even when their result delivery is suppressed: `get_state.hasOwnedRunningWork` reports these independently of conversation quiescence (`isSettled` keeps its native meaning). A successful response acknowledges shutdown; wait for host exit `0` to confirm persistence. Persistence failures produce a notice and nonzero host exit. Stopping a socket client is only a disconnect, never an implicit host shutdown.
+
 Protocol v1 stdout frames are a single JSON object followed by `\n`. The server caps each physical stdout frame at 1 MiB, including the newline. Inbound frames are always one unchunked JSONL object; clients SHOULD keep them within the advertised physical-frame limit. Input is not reassembled from `rpc_chunk` frames.
 
 The initial ready frame uses protocol v1 and advertises the opt-in lossless transport:
